  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes several ways circular RNAs can influence gene expression and protein activity. Across the panels, circular RNAs are shown as ring-shaped molecules that act through different mechanisms rather than serving only as passive RNA products.\n\nOne major role is as microRNA sponges: a circRNA can bind a microRNA, such as miR-1231, and sequester it away from its normal mRNA target. Another mechanism is direct interaction with mRNA, where a circRNA associates with a transcript and appears to affect its stability or translation. The figure also shows that some circRNAs can themselves be translated, producing a short circRNA-specific peptide.\n\nThe lower part emphasizes protein-related functions. CircRNAs can bind proteins as “sponges,” recruit proteins to nucleic acids, or act as scaffolds that bring multiple proteins together. In one example, recruitment of TET1 is linked to demethylation, suggesting regulation at the chromatin or RNA level. Another example shows enhancer-like activity, where a circRNA seems to cooperate with snRNP and RNA polymerase II to promote transcription.\n\nOverall, the image presents circRNAs as multifunctional regulators acting through RNA, protein, and translation-based pathways.",
